      "reasoning": "Student’s result is essentially correct; confirm with precise calculation using a calculator tool. Provide final answer with formula breakdown and exam tip as required.",
      "content": "Exactly, the tension comes out to about 29 N. Doing the calculation precisely:\n[TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"3*9.8 - 3*0.11\") -> 29.07]\n\n**Final Answer:** $\\displaystyle T = 29.07\\text{ N}$ (rounded to $29.1\\text{ N}$ is acceptable).\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $T = mg - ma$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $T$: tension in the string (N)\n  * $m$: mass of the falling block (kg)\n  * $g$: acceleration due to gravity ($9.8\\,\\text{m·s}^{-2}$ on Earth)\n  * $a$: acceleration of the block (found from the motion, $0.11\\,\\text{m·s}^{-2}$)\n* **Why we use it:** The block’s weight tries to pull it down, while the string pulls up. The difference between these forces produces the net force that accelerates the block (Newton’s 2nd law). Solving for $T$ isolates the string’s pull.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Write the free‑body diagram first and label each force. Then state the net‑force equation before substituting numbers – this earns you the method mark and avoids sign errors.\n"
    }
